Date: Sun, 6 Sep 2026 23:06:25 +0300
Subject: [PATCH 61/71] Keep column padding, which a tokenizer already
 collapses

`normalize` trimmed trailing whitespace from every line on the theory that
`Format-Table -AutoSize` pads each row out to the widest value and that padding is
free tokens. Re-priced with a real tokenizer instead of the bytes/4 estimate, it is
close enough to free that removing it does not pay.

A BPE tokenizer merges runs of spaces: 32 spaces are one token and 121 are two, not
the 30 that bytes/4 predicts. Measured with `o200k_base`, which tracks the provider
to 0.98x over the outputs of session 2026-09-05T18-32-48 and so stands in for GLM's
tokenizer, the trim is worth 394 tokens across every read and listing in that
session -- 1.3%, about three hundredths of a cent. The 30.8% that pass really
removes is `filter_artifact_paths` dropping `node_modules` path text; on the one
recursive listing that dominates the session it goes 9,409 -> 9,099 -> 950 tokens,
and only the last step is the trim's neighbour doing the work.

Against that, the trim rewrites text the model may be about to quote back. A patch
context line survives it today because `seek_sequence` falls back to `trim_end` and
then to `trim` matching, so nothing was breaking; it is simply not worth altering
output the model reads in order to save three hundredths of a cent.

Escape-sequence stripping and carriage-return handling stay. Neither is whitespace
and neither compresses, and the Windows line terminator is one character a line that
would otherwise end every line of output on this machine.

The estimator is what made this look worthwhile. `approx_token_count` is bytes/4,
which is 0.98x of provider truth in aggregate and on plain file reads, 1.07x on
padded tables -- accurate enough to attribute a bill between items, and wrong by
roughly ten times when the thing being priced only removes whitespace. That also
means the "7.8% reduction over 892 outputs" measured for the previous commit is
inflated in its lossless share; its artifact-filter share is sound.

